|~ qlang operand catalog — typeClassifier family. `:type` is the
   canonical kind reader: it returns the value's kind, a tag — its
   outermost tag, or the kind of the core its literal implies.
   Asking what a value is means composing that reader with `eq` —
   `| type | eq ::string` — so the family holds one operand.
   Identity rides the value's JS-header slot, so a Map carrying a
   `:kind ::Foo` field answers `::map` — the field is data, and
   `::Foo{…}` is how a value declares what it is. ~|

:type
  |~~ Returns the value's kind, a tag. For tagged values (errors,
      conduits, binding records, tagged-instances, builtin descriptors,
      quotes, sets) it is the tag stamped on the value's JS-header
      `TAG_HEADER_SYMBOL` slot; for every other value the kind of
      the core its literal implies (`::number`, `::string`,
      `::keyword`, `::tag`, `::vec`, `::map`, …), named under
      the prefix `qlang/` and written short. `type | docs` reads
      the kind's page.

      `result !| type` is the user-facing path to an error's
      tag identity — the materialised descriptor exposed by
      `!|` is a Map with the per-site `::ErrorTag` riding on
      the JS-header slot, and `type` reads through the same
      invariant every other tagged value-class shares.

      ~(42 | type | eq ::number)
      ~("hi" | type | eq ::string)
      ~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
      ~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)
      ~(::conduit[[] ~(42)] | type | eq ::conduit)
      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/type
            :category :typeClassifier
            :subject :any
            :returns :keyword
   }
